  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "**This is the easy version of the problem. In this version, you may find any permutation that satisfies the conditions.\n\nThis problem is worth 100100100 points in divisions 333 and 444, and 606060 points in divisions 111 and 222.**\n\nYou are given an integer NNN (N≥3N\\geq 3N≥3).\n\nFind a permutation†^\\dagger† PPP of the integers 111 to NNN such that Pi≠iP_i \\neq iPi​=i for every index iii, and\n\nmax⁡i=1N(Pi⊕i)\\max_{i=1}^N \\left( P_i \\oplus i\\right)i=1maxN​(Pi​⊕i)is minimized.\n\nHere, ⊕\\oplus⊕ denotes the bitwise XOR operation.\n\nIf there are multiple permutations that minimize this quantity, you may print any of them.\n\n†^\\dagger†A permutation of the integers 111 to NNN is an array of length NNN that contains every integer from 111 to NNN.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nThe first and only line of each test case contains a single integer NNN.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line NNN space-separated integers denoting the permutation you found.\n\nIf there are multiple possible answers, you may print any of them.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤10001 \\leq T \\leq 10001≤T≤1000",
      "3≤N≤5⋅1053 \\leq N \\leq 5\\cdot 10^53≤N≤5⋅105",
      "The sum of NNN across all tests won't exceed 5⋅1055\\cdot 10^55⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n3\n4\n6",
        "output": "3 1 2\n4 3 2 1\n2 3 1 5 6 4",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: For P=[3,1,2]P = [3, 1, 2]P=[3,1,2], we obtain:\n\nP1⊕1=3⊕1=2P_1\\oplus 1 = 3\\oplus 1 = 2P1​⊕1=3⊕1=2\nP2⊕2=1⊕2=3P_2\\oplus 2 = 1\\oplus 2 = 3P2​⊕2=1⊕2=3\nP3⊕3=2⊕3=1P_3\\oplus 3 = 2\\oplus 3 = 1P3​⊕3=2⊕3=1\n\nThe maximum among these is 333.\nIt can be proved that no permutation of length 333 has Pi≠iP_i \\neq iPi​=i and Pi⊕i<3P_i\\oplus i \\lt 3Pi​⊕i<3 for every index iii.\nTest case 222: The minimum possible maximum XOR is 555."
